WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.269
Some objects are too faint to see in a single picture.

2
00:00:04.319 --> 00:00:06.875
Today we make one appear out of nothing.

3
00:00:07.533 --> 00:00:12.126
This field holds WISE 0855, the coldest brown dwarf we know.

4
00:00:12.176 --> 00:00:18.858
It is colder than ice, about minus 20 degrees Celsius, and only seven light-years away.

5
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:24.010
Look at the centre of any single picture. Nothing. Just grainy noise.

6
00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:27.975
The object is there, but its light is weaker than the noise.

7
00:00:28.633 --> 00:00:31.025
Here is the trick. Noise is random:

8
00:00:31.075 --> 00:00:35.979
in one picture a pixel is a bit too bright, in the next a bit too dark.

9
00:00:36.029 --> 00:00:38.700
The object is always in the same place.

10
00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:43.933
If we average many pictures, the noise cancels out, but the object adds up.

11
00:00:44.567 --> 00:00:50.771
Average 16 pictures, and the noise drops four times, because of the square root of 16.

12
00:00:50.821 --> 00:00:52.517
This is called stacking.

13
00:00:53.167 --> 00:00:56.612
Now switch to the stacked passes. And there it is:

14
00:00:56.662 --> 00:01:00.667
a faint dot in the centre. It was hiding in every picture.

15
00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:04.470
Now watch the dot from pass to pass. It moves:

16
00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:07.830
about four and a half arcseconds in half a year.

17
00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:11.050
A nearby object, moving against the far stars.

18
00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:15.733
Why did we choose this colour band, 4.4 to 5 micrometres?

19
00:01:15.783 --> 00:01:20.317
Because a cold brown dwarf gives off almost all its light there.

20
00:01:20.367 --> 00:01:23.325
In the shorter colours it is nearly black.

21
00:01:23.967 --> 00:01:24.676
Click on it:

22
00:01:24.726 --> 00:01:28.724
the site measures the motion and compares it with the catalogue.

23
00:01:28.774 --> 00:01:31.317
They agree to a fraction of an arcsecond.

24
00:01:31.967 --> 00:01:36.292
Your turn: the mission, The invisible neighbour, hides the name.

25
00:01:36.342 --> 00:01:38.617
Use the stacked passes to find it.

26
00:01:39.267 --> 00:01:39.833
Remember:

27
00:01:39.883 --> 00:01:44.213
when something is too faint, take more pictures and add them up.

28
00:01:44.263 --> 00:01:46.267
Astronomers do this every day.
